WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.777
The saying is, you know, water is life.

00:00:08.554 --> 00:00:10.478
We're a water-born people.

00:00:10.478 --> 00:00:11.670
We depend on it.

00:00:12.989 --> 00:00:16.279
This fishing spot is ancient.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:19.380
The Mohawk — the ​​Kanienʼkehá꞉ka
— were fishing here

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.297
thousands of years before the first 
European settlers arrived.

00:00:25.761 --> 00:00:31.256
This is Akwesasne where the Mohawk have fished
since long before this border

00:00:31.256 --> 00:00:34.199
cut their nation in half.

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:36.180
Fishing in this community is important.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.690
I know for our grandparents it was 
a way of life.

00:00:41.237 --> 00:00:45.514
I believe in having those traditions passed
down so that they never are forgotten.

00:00:45.514 --> 00:00:47.448
That one's huge!

00:00:47.448 --> 00:00:52.391
Seeing those little boys and girls smiling
and catching fish and enjoying themselves...

00:00:53.842 --> 00:00:55.628
That's the best.

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:03.306
If you head upriver, just a couple 
of miles from this spot

00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:06.331
you’ll find two abandoned industrial sites.

00:01:06.331 --> 00:01:11.742
This is the spot where poisonous chemicals
made their way into the river...

00:01:11.742 --> 00:01:13.656
into the fish...

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:16.600
and into the Mohawk people.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.430
Forty years later, they're still carrying
that poison.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.447
And chances are, you are too.

00:01:30.454 --> 00:01:33.400
People used to live on the islands themselves.

00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:35.727
Totally self-sufficient.

00:01:35.727 --> 00:01:39.258
My grandfather used to live on St. Regis Island.

00:01:39.258 --> 00:01:42.160
He's the one that introduced me to fishing.

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:44.848
We would row across the river.

00:01:44.848 --> 00:01:48.000
The shorelines were covered with weed beds...

00:01:48.905 --> 00:01:52.791
and it was a task to get a rowboat through the weed beds.

00:01:52.791 --> 00:01:55.860
We'd catch perch, sunfish, rock bass.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.084
He showed me how to clean fish...

00:01:58.084 --> 00:02:00.450
explain to me what it was all about.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.920
My grandmother used to take me out to the
berry gardens

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:07.240
and we picked raspberries and strawberries,
blueberries out there.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:08.828
We had potatoes. Vegetables. 

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:11.127
My grandfather had a herd of cows.

00:02:11.127 --> 00:02:13.570
Just about everything we needed was out there.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:15.520
I remember that.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:21.170
In the 1950s, the US and Canadian governments
started a massive project

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.746
to connect the Great Lakes with 
the Atlantic Ocean

00:02:23.746 --> 00:02:26.373
by widening and deepening 
the St. Lawrence River. 

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.770
The Seaway changed the geography of Akwesasne.

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:38.141
"All the trees on the affected area 
must be cut down."

00:02:38.141 --> 00:02:40.069
There was an apple orchard there.

00:02:40.069 --> 00:02:41.627
Big apple orchard.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:44.144
Old man John owned that.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.778
And they said they were going 
to build a bridge across.

00:02:48.778 --> 00:02:52.620
They hadn't even consulted him 
until the equipment came up.

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:56.772
The Seaway’s locks and dams allowed giant
container ships like these

00:02:56.772 --> 00:02:58.900
to navigate to the Great Lakes

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.080
and the dams supplied electricity

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.880
which the state of New York promised to sell
to companies for cheap.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.969
"Every cent of expenditures will be recovered
through total charges

00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:11.222
and revenue from the sale of power."

00:03:11.222 --> 00:03:15.130
"In the long run, it will cost the people
of the two nations nothing."

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:20.350
The press coverage focused exclusively on
the benefits for Canadians and Americans.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.247
But there was a third nation whose people
were affected.

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:29.209
A lot of our older people couldn't speak English.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:34.450
They couldn't understand why, 
how this could happen.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:35.819
How could a people do this?

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:37.468
How could engineers do this?

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:44.630
When the Seaway went through, 
they weren't informed.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:47.180
They weren't asked what they thought about
this.

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:53.876
"The power to run the machines, the motors,
to light the signs, the homes...

00:03:53.876 --> 00:03:57.918
supply energy for the thousand and one needs
of an expanding economy."

00:03:57.918 --> 00:04:00.448
There were nine islands that were flooded.

00:04:00.448 --> 00:04:02.227
There used to be a big inlet. 

00:04:02.227 --> 00:04:03.660
It was a swampy area.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.114
They filled that in.

00:04:05.114 --> 00:04:07.063
They said, oh, we'll do you a favor.

00:04:08.062 --> 00:04:10.591
What about the muskrats?

00:04:10.591 --> 00:04:11.810
What about the beaver?

00:04:12.905 --> 00:04:15.112
They're gone. They're gone now.

00:04:15.112 --> 00:04:18.456
"Thus, man is conquering another 
of nature’s challenges."

00:04:18.456 --> 00:04:22.690
"Moving the Atlantic Ocean to the entire
 mid-continent empire."

00:04:28.766 --> 00:04:32.981
When the frogs come out and they start to
make noise

00:04:32.981 --> 00:04:37.754
that for me indicates that the water and the
ground temperature is right to do the spearfishing.

00:04:41.012 --> 00:04:43.386
This is it, this is the magic spot.

00:04:43.823 --> 00:04:46.087
So much life happens here.

00:04:52.848 --> 00:04:54.478
When you enter the water

00:04:54.478 --> 00:04:57.438
it's not really good to walk where 
the fish are doing their thing.

00:04:57.438 --> 00:05:00.710
That's their spawning bed, that's where 
they want to lay their eggs.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.740
So it's pretty precious space there.

00:05:03.287 --> 00:05:06.697
You've got to be gentle and you've gotta 
be selective.

00:05:06.697 --> 00:05:11.223
Just recognizing that it's a gift.

00:05:11.223 --> 00:05:14.520
You should just respect it.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.169
Just having that gratefulness that we're still
able to do it, that's what I always get to.

00:05:19.169 --> 00:05:21.151
We're so lucky.

00:05:21.151 --> 00:05:23.572
We're really, really lucky.

00:05:23.572 --> 00:05:26.049
This is what I know.

00:05:26.049 --> 00:05:30.159
I’m the oldest son, so I have little brothers
and I teach them this stuff.

00:05:34.839 --> 00:05:40.740
The Mohawk creation story says at the beginning
of time, the whole world was covered in water

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.401
and there was no land.

00:05:42.401 --> 00:05:45.159
That's when Skywoman falls from the sky.

00:05:45.159 --> 00:05:48.249
She falls to the earth with a child 
in her belly.

00:05:48.249 --> 00:05:52.750
The ducks and the loons and the geese decided
that they would try to help her from falling

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:55.039
straight into the waters.

00:05:55.039 --> 00:05:59.539
In the end, it was the giant turtle who said
that he could put the soil on his back.

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:01.220
She could land on his back.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:09.086
And as she danced in the circle, as she shuffled
her feet on the dirt, it expanded and expanded.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:14.749
Our people give thanks to the water.

00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:17.599
They see it as an essential part 
of who we are.

00:06:19.543 --> 00:06:24.479
New York’s plan to lure industry upstate
with cheap electricity, it worked.

00:06:24.479 --> 00:06:28.289
Two major corporations: General Motors and
Reynolds Metal

00:06:28.289 --> 00:06:34.229
built factories in the mostly white town of
Massena, right next to Akwesasne.

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:36.103
According to the local newspaper

00:06:36.103 --> 00:06:40.392
Massena’s politicians and business leaders 
reacted “with joy”.

00:06:40.392 --> 00:06:45.690
But for the Mohawk of Akwesasne, the news
meant something else entirely.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:49.224
Soon after the new factories opened in the
late 50s

00:06:49.224 --> 00:06:52.105
Mohawk farmers on Cornwall Island started
to notice their calves

00:06:52.105 --> 00:06:53.805
weren't growing as well.

00:06:53.805 --> 00:06:57.069
Then the cattle started 
mysteriously dying off.

00:06:57.069 --> 00:07:00.793
Later, it turned out that 400 pounds 
of fluoride ash

00:07:00.793 --> 00:07:03.650
was spewing out of the new Reynolds' plant 
each hour.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:08.138
Veterinarians discovered it had poisoned the
grass and eroded the cows teeth

00:07:08.138 --> 00:07:11.267
making it too painful for them to eat or drink.

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.501
They were starving.

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:17.076
If you look across the history of settler-colonialism

00:07:17.076 --> 00:07:21.363
in areas where indigenous people remained
and still lived

00:07:21.363 --> 00:07:26.006
corporations working under the laws
of the United States were able to dump

00:07:26.006 --> 00:07:29.745
the most dangerous chemicals and pollutants there. 

00:07:29.745 --> 00:07:34.939
While I was in early practice doing home
births here...

00:07:34.939 --> 00:07:37.629
there was a cluster of babies born.

00:07:37.629 --> 00:07:45.549
One baby born with an omphalocele, which is
where the intestines grow outside the belly.

00:07:45.549 --> 00:07:49.619
Another was a baby born with no intestines at all

00:07:49.619 --> 00:07:52.910
and we had to let that baby die. 

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:58.300
As an anecdote, it still gives you a sense of...
where is this heading?

00:07:58.300 --> 00:08:04.259
"That is polychlorinated biphenyls which are
used in a wide variety of manufactured products."

00:08:04.259 --> 00:08:09.189
PCBs started showing up in American news stories
in the late 1960s

00:08:09.189 --> 00:08:11.833
when they were discovered in salmon and poultry.

00:08:11.833 --> 00:08:16.499
At the time, there was just one manufacturer
of PCBs in the US:

00:08:16.499 --> 00:08:19.695
The Monsanto Company of St. Louis.

00:08:19.695 --> 00:08:22.800
They marketed the chemical 
under the name Aroclor

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.560
and sold it to other companies who then used
it to make products like shower curtains,

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:30.595
paint, and electrical equipment.

00:08:30.595 --> 00:08:33.695
Including the electrical equipment in those
plants

00:08:33.695 --> 00:08:37.239
the ones GM and Reynolds had built right next to Akwesasne.

00:08:38.339 --> 00:08:43.409
In 1971, a Monsanto representative told the
New York Times

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:45.759
that the company was studying 
the chemical’s effects

00:08:45.759 --> 00:08:47.808
on rats and dogs.

00:08:47.808 --> 00:08:52.360
So far, they said, “no ill effects had yet
been detected.”

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:56.830
But Monsanto’s internal documents from that
time tell a different story.

00:08:56.830 --> 00:09:00.699
They knew that PCBs 
caused liver damage in rats.

00:09:00.699 --> 00:09:06.970
They knew that PCB exposure had sent workers
to the hospital with symptoms of hepatitis.

00:09:06.970 --> 00:09:12.110
And in 1970 one of Monsanto’s own studies
on PCBs in fish

00:09:12.110 --> 00:09:18.980
showed that doses of PCB "which were believed
to be OK produced 100% kill."

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:20.967
All the fish died.

00:09:23.266 --> 00:09:29.374
Eating a certain food isn't just putting the
stuff in your mouth.

00:09:29.374 --> 00:09:35.040
It's what goes on all around that moment of
consumption.

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.180
My mom would tell me stories about how my
father and his family would have these fish

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:41.709
skinning competitions.

00:09:41.709 --> 00:09:46.160
And it makes me so happy to think about how
that was in itself

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.240
like a way that we created community and family
for each other

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.480
and supported each other.

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.509
With really good friends or really good family
members…

00:09:55.509 --> 00:09:57.110
We've known them for a long time.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:58.230
We trust them.

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.440
We know that they respect our independence
and our consent.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.621
In indigenous ways of understanding the world

00:10:05.621 --> 00:10:10.920
we can use that way of thinking about relationships 
as a model.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:15.843
What would it mean to have 
relationships of trust with fish?

00:10:15.843 --> 00:10:18.901
Relationships of reciprocity with a forest?

00:10:18.901 --> 00:10:21.560
Or consent with a plant?

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:27.178
Our community is not just the humans, but
all of our interdependent relationships.

00:10:27.178 --> 00:10:30.860
When we teach our children about giving thanks

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:35.930
that's what we’re trying to do is get them
to see that they’re not just fish,

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:37.860
but they’re, fish! [said with emphasis]

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:41.645
And these fish help us to maintain our life.

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:51.230
In 1981, two pits full of PCB sludge were
discovered

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:54.069
behind the GM factory near Akwesasne

00:10:54.069 --> 00:10:57.562
prompting scientists to start testing
local wildlife.

00:10:57.562 --> 00:11:03.386
At the time, chickens with PCB concentrations 
of more than 5 parts per million

00:11:03.386 --> 00:11:06.000
couldn’t be sold in supermarkets

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:09.451
but most of the fish in Akwesasne 
had levels much higher. 

00:11:09.451 --> 00:11:13.170
30 parts per million is considered toxic waste.

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:21.120
Researchers tested one snapping turtle with
a PCB concentration of 835 parts per million.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:27.304
It turned out, both GM and Reynolds had been
dumping PCB waste into the St. Lawrence River

00:11:27.304 --> 00:11:28.577
for decades.

00:11:28.577 --> 00:11:32.629
We were just straight up told not to eat fish,
that we would die.

00:11:32.629 --> 00:11:36.519
They were telling us the tomatoes that were
coming out of our garden shouldn’t be eaten

00:11:36.519 --> 00:11:37.519
by children.

00:11:37.519 --> 00:11:41.490
A lot of people just stopped farming altogether.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:45.220
I remember talking to an older lady from the
island here

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:47.180
and she actually burst into tears.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:55.149
And she said, “When I go down to the shoreline
to look for Sweet Flake, it's not there."

00:11:55.149 --> 00:11:56.699
"I look for my Sweet grass.

00:11:56.699 --> 00:11:58.385
It's not there."

00:11:59.261 --> 00:12:02.321
"What have they done to this place?”

00:12:03.361 --> 00:12:09.429
The plants, the animals, the ecosystems are
thought of as part of the bonds

00:12:09.429 --> 00:12:10.848
part of the friendships.

00:12:10.848 --> 00:12:17.910
And imagine if somebody were to take something
that was really important to a friendship

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:21.579
a family relationship that you have
and just pollute it

00:12:21.579 --> 00:12:24.357
and just damage or destroy it.

00:12:24.357 --> 00:12:26.360
That hits us hard, right?

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:28.373
That's devastating.

00:12:28.373 --> 00:12:31.991
But see, this is what Native people have been
through with colonialism

00:12:31.991 --> 00:12:37.490
a systematic, comprehensive destruction of
all of those touchstones in our society

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:44.412
those environments that formed the contexts
through which our best relationships unfolded.

00:12:44.412 --> 00:12:47.430
We lost pieces of our language, of our value
system

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:49.339
with the river because of our disconnect

00:12:49.339 --> 00:12:51.139
because of these fish advisories.

00:12:51.139 --> 00:12:55.110
And so right now, I can't recommend people
to consume fish because that's our policy.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:59.444
But, personally, we have to consume fish so
we know their names

00:12:59.444 --> 00:13:01.723
and we reconnect with them.

00:13:01.723 --> 00:13:06.709
There was an existing relationship they had
with everything that water system provided.

00:13:06.709 --> 00:13:08.640
It was part of their life.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:13.886
And it was taken away. It changed.

00:13:13.886 --> 00:13:15.946
All of a sudden, it wasn’t there.

00:13:15.946 --> 00:13:21.220
In the decades since PCBs were discovered,
scientists documented the scope of the PCB

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.850
problems outside of Awkwesasne.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:28.740
People all over North America were told not
to eat the fish from the rivers, streams,

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:30.872
and lakes where they lived.

00:13:30.872 --> 00:13:36.720
Scientists also learned that PCBs can move
from soil into the air through a process called

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:38.839
volatilization.

00:13:38.839 --> 00:13:43.510
Which means that even if you don't live near
a PCB dump you can still be exposed through

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.548
the air that you breathe.

00:13:45.548 --> 00:13:51.649
In 2013, the UN’s International Agency for
Research in Cancer stated officially:

00:13:51.649 --> 00:13:54.999
"PCBs are carcinogenic to humans”

00:13:54.999 --> 00:13:58.589
and they are “probably in the body of every
human” on earth.

00:14:00.816 --> 00:14:07.110
I can't do too much given our current reality.

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:10.961
But I can go out there in the morning 
and sing.

00:14:10.961 --> 00:14:12.559
[singing in Mohawk]

00:14:26.108 --> 00:14:27.602
To be...

00:14:33.638 --> 00:14:35.220
To be grateful.

00:14:39.764 --> 00:14:42.220
You come from the ground up.

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:45.099
You give thanks to everything.

00:14:45.099 --> 00:14:54.000
[speaking in Mohawk] We will put our minds
together as one and acknowledge the fish.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:59.297
The roots, the trees, the plants, the skies,
the wind, the stars.

00:15:05.475 --> 00:15:06.546
On wha nagula.

00:15:07.257 --> 00:15:08.257
One mind.

00:15:09.943 --> 00:15:15.894
[singing in Mohawk] One fish, two fish, three
fish

00:15:15.894 --> 00:15:24.067
four fish, five fish, five fish...

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:29.940
The river has a right to community and to
build community around itself.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:35.735
And I think a lot about how that's our responsibility
as Ongweoweh people to facilitate that.

00:15:37.487 --> 00:15:43.600
There was a little boy ice fishing and he
was putting the fish in a big plastic bucket.

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:48.870
And we saw him at the end of the day, take
his bucket and run home with it.

00:15:49.719 --> 00:15:54.580
And I wondered, what is his mother going to
say when she sees the fish

00:15:54.580 --> 00:15:57.089
her little son caught?

00:15:58.567 --> 00:16:00.474
She’s going to be proud of him.

